readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|I trowe nat now that I be ''unparygal'' to the strokes of fortune.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iii. prose 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|My knaverie growes ''unperegall.''
{{right|''Marston'', Dutch Courtezan, iv. 5.}}}}

'''unparfit†''', ''a.'' A Middle English form of ''unperfect.''

'''unparliamentarily''' (un-pär-lị-men′tạ-ri-li), ''adv.'' In an unparliamentary manner.

'''unparliamentariness''' (un-pär-lị-men′tạ-ri-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being unparliamentary.

'''unparliamentary''' (un-pär-lị-men′tạ-ri), ''a.'' Contrary to the usages or rules of proceeding in Parliament or in any legislative (or by extension deliberative) body; not such as can be used or uttered in Parliament or any legislative body: as, ''unparliamentary'' language.

{{smaller block|Having failed, too, in getting supplies by ''unparliamentary'' methods, Charles “consulted with Sir Robert Cotton what was to be done.”
{{right|''Carlyle'', Cromwell’s Letters and Speeches, Int., iv.}}}}

'''unparroted''' (un-par′ọt-ed), ''a.'' Not repeated by rote as if by a parrot. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Her sentiments were ''unparroted'' and unstudied.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, i. 207. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''unpartial†''' (un-pär′shạl), ''a.'' Not partial; impartial.

{{smaller block|I weighed the matter which you committed into my hands with my most ''unpartial'' and farthest reach of reason.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, v.}}}}

'''unpartially''' (un-pär′shạl-i), ''adv.'' Impartially.

{{smaller block|Deal ''unpartially'' with thine own heart.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Balm of Gilead, § 12.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Pay her the debt you owe her, and ''unpay'' the villany you have done her. {{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., ii. 1. 130.}}}}

'''unpayable''' (un-pā′a̤-bl), ''a.'' Incapable of being paid. ''South'', Sermons, X. ix.

'''unpeace†''' (un-pēs′), ''n.'' [< ME. ''unpece''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''peace''.] Absence of peace; dispeace.

'''unpeaceable''' (un-pē′sa̤-bl), ''a.'' Not peaceable; quarrelsome.

{{smaller block|Away, ''unpeaceable'' dog, or I’ll spurn thee hence! {{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., i. 1. 280.}}}}

'''unpeaceableness''' (un-pē′sa̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The state of being unpeaceable; unquietness; quarrelsomeness. ''Mountagu.''

'''unpeaceful''' (un-pēs′fu̇l), ''a.'' Not pacific or peaceful; unquiet; disturbed. ''Milton'', Ans. to Eikon Basilike, xviii.

'''unpedigreed''' (un-ped′i-grēd), ''a.'' Not distinguished by a pedigree. ''R. Pollok.''

'''unpeerable''' (un-pēr′a̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''peer''<sup>2</sup> + ''-able''.] Such that no peer can be found; incomparable.

'''unpeered''' (un-pērd′), ''a.'' Having no peer or equal; unequaled.

{{smaller block|Such an ''unpeer’d'' excellence. {{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, I., v. 1.}}}}

'''unpeg''' (un-peg′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''peg''.] To pull out the peg or pegs from; open by removing a peg or pegs.

{{smaller block|''Unpeg'' the basket on the house’s top,<br>Let the birds fly. {{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 4. 193.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''unpen'''<sup>1</sup> (un-pen′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''pen''<sup>1</sup>.] To let out or release from being penned or dammed up; set free from a pen or confinement.
{{smaller block|If a man ''unpens'' another’s water. {{right|''Blackstone.''}}}}

'''unpen'''<sup>2</sup>† (un-pen′), ''v. t.'' To deprive of feathers.
{{smaller block|A new convert is like a bird newly entered into a net; . . . when, by busy and disturbed flutterings, she discomposes the order of it, she is entangled and ''unpenned'', and made a prey to her treacherous enemy.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 108.}}}}

'''unpenetrable''' (un-pen′ẹ̄-tra-bl), ''a.'' Impenetrable. ''Holland'', tr. of Pliny, viii. 25. [Rare.]

'''unpenitent''' (un-pen′i-te̤nt), ''a.'' Impenitent ''Sandys'', Paraphrase of Job, p. 52. [Rare.]

'''unpensioned''' (un-pen′sho̤nd), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not pensioned; not rewarded by a pension: as, an ''unpensioned'' soldier.—'''2.''' Not kept in pay; not held in dependence by a pension. ''Byron'', Mazeppa, iv.

'''unpeople''' (un-pē′pl), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''people''.] To deprive of people; deprive of inhabitants; depopulate; dispeople.
{{smaller block|I’ll ''unpeople'' Egypt. {{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., i. 5. 78.}}
''Unpeopled'' offices, untrodden stones.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., i. 2. 69.}}
They have ''unpeopl’d'' the Kingdome by expulsion of so many thousands. {{right|''Milton'', Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

'''unpeppered''' (un-pep′ėrd), ''a.'' Unseasoned; not piquant. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye Novel-Readers, such as relish most
Plain Nature’s feast, ''unpepper’d'' with a Ghost.</poem>
{{right|''Colman'', Vagaries Vindicated, p. 203. (''Davies.'')}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''unperceivable''' (un-pėr-sē′vạ-bl), ''a.'' Incapable of being perceived; not perceptible. ''South'', Sermons, IV. ix.

'''unperceivably''' (un-pėr-sē′vạ-bli), ''adv.'' In an unperceived manner; imperceptibly.

'''unperceived''' (un-pėr-sēvd′), ''a.'' Not perceived; not heeded; not observed; not noticed.
{{smaller block|An invigorating and purifying emanation, which, unseen and ''unperceived'', elevates the debased affections.
{{right|''Isaac Taylor'', Nat. Hist. Enthusiasm, p. 68.}}}}

'''unperceivedly''' (un-pėr-sē′ved-li), ''adv.'' So as not to be perceived; imperceptibly. ''Boyle'', Works, V. 260.

'''unperceptible†''' (un-pėr-sep′ti-bl), ''a.'' Imperceptible. ''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 888.

'''unperch''' (un-pėrch′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''perch''.] To drive from a perch. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Either rowse the Deere, or ''vnpearch'' the Phesant.
{{right|''Lyly'', Euphues, Anat. of Wit, p. 114.}}}}

'''unperegal†''', ''a.'' Same as ''unparegal''.

'''unperfect''' (un-pėr′fekt), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unperfit'', ''unparfit'', ''unperfight''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''perfect''.] Not perfect. {{smaller|(''a'') Not consummated, finished, or completed; undeveloped.}}
{{smaller block|Recharde hermyte reherces a dredfull tale of ''vn-perfitte'' contrecyone that a haly mane Cesarius tellys in ensample.
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 6.}}
Thine eyes did see mine ''unperfect'' substance.
{{right|Ps. cxxxix. 16 [R. V.].}}
<poem>Then is there monarchy
''Unperfect'' yet. ''Middleton'', Game at Chess, Ind.</poem>
’Tis finished what ''unperfect'' was before.
{{right|''Ford'', Ben Jonson.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Deficient; imperfect; faulty; lacking in something.}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>The Pope asso[i]led hym ther benyngly,
When declared hade hys dedes ''vnperfight.''</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 5225.}}}}
{{smaller block|An ''unperfect'' actor.{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xxiii.}}}}

'''unperfect†''' (un-pėr′fekt), ''v. t.'' To leave unfinished. ''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, iii.

'''unperfection†''' (un-pėr-fek′shọn), ''n.'' [ME. ''unperfeccioun''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''perfection.''] Imperfection. ''Wyclif'', Ecclus. xxxviii. 31.

'''unperfectly†''' (un-pėr′fekt-li), ''adv.'' Imperfectly. ''Tyndale'', Ans. to Sir T. More, etc. (Parker Soc., 1850), p. 207.

'''unperfectness''' (un-pėr′fekt-nes), ''n.'' Imperfection.
{{smaller block|Being of my ''unperfectness'' unworthy of your friendship.{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, i.}}}}

'''unperformed''' (un-pėr-fôrmd′), ''a.'' Not performed; not done; not executed; not fulfilled; hence, not represented on the stage; unacted: as, the business remains ''unperformed''; an ''unperformed'' promise; the play remained ''unperformed.''
{{smaller block|This voyage, ''unperform’d'' by living man.{{right|''Cowper'', Odyssey, x.}}}}

'''unperishable†''' (un-per′ish-a-bl), ''a.'' Not perishable; imperishable. ''Spectator'', No. 537.

'''unperishably†''' (un-per′ish-ạ-bli), ''adv.'' Imperishably.

'''unperishing''' (un-per′ish-ing), ''a.'' Not perishing; lasting; durable.
{{smaller block|Her great sire’s ''unperishing'' abode.{{right|''Cowper'', Iliad, xix.}}}}
